NNAAMMEE
     ssddiiffff — side-by-side diff

SSYYNNOOPPSSIISS
     ssddiiffff [--aabbddiillssttHHWW] [--II _r_e_g_e_x_p] [--oo _o_u_t_f_i_l_e] [--ww _w_i_d_t_h] _f_i_l_e_1 _f_i_l_e_2

DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
     ssddiiffff displays two files side by side, with any differences between the
     two highlighted as follows: new lines are marked with ‘>’; deleted lines
     are marked with ‘<’; and changed lines are marked with ‘|’.

     ssddiiffff can also be used to interactively merge two files, prompting at
     each set of differences.  See the --oo option for an explanation.

     The options are:

     --ll ----lleefftt--ccoolluummnn
             Only print the left column for identical lines.

     --oo ----oouuttppuutt _o_u_t_f_i_l_e
             Interactively merge _f_i_l_e_1 and _f_i_l_e_2 into _o_u_t_f_i_l_e.  In this mode,
             the user is prompted for each set of differences.  See EDITOR and
             VISUAL, below, for details of which editor, if any, is invoked.

             The commands are as follows:

             ll | 11   Choose left set of diffs.

             rr | 22   Choose right set of diffs.

             ss       Silent mode – identical lines are not printed.

             vv       Verbose mode – identical lines are printed.

             ee       Start editing an empty file, which will be merged into
                     _o_u_t_f_i_l_e upon exiting the editor.

             ee ll     Start editing file with left set of diffs.

             ee rr     Start editing file with right set of diffs.

             ee bb     Start editing file with both sets of diffs.

             qq       Quit ssddiiffff.

     --ss ----ssuupppprreessss--ccoommmmoonn--lliinneess
             Skip identical lines.

     --ww ----wwiiddtthh _w_i_d_t_h
             Print a maximum of _w_i_d_t_h characters on each line.  The default is
             130 characters.

     Options passed to diff(1) are:

     --aa ----tteexxtt
             Treat _f_i_l_e_1 and _f_i_l_e_2 as text files.

     --bb ----iiggnnoorree--ssppaaccee--cchhaannggee
             Ignore trailing blank spaces.

     --dd ----mmiinniimmaall
             Minimize diff size.

     --II ----iiggnnoorree--mmaattcchhiinngg--lliinneess _r_e_g_e_x_p
             Ignore line changes matching _r_e_g_e_x_p.  All lines in the change
             must match _r_e_g_e_x_p for the change to be ignored.

     --ii ----iiggnnoorree--ccaassee
             Do a case-insensitive comparison.

     --tt ----eexxppaanndd--ttaabbss
             Expand tabs to spaces.

     --WW ----iiggnnoorree--aallll--ssppaaccee
             Ignore all spaces.

     --BB ----iiggnnoorree--bbllaannkk--lliinneess
             Ignore blank lines.

     --EE ----iiggnnoorree--ttaabb--eexxppaannssiioonn
             Treat tabs and eight spaces as the same.

     --HH ----ssppeeeedd--llaarrggee--ffiilleess
             Assume scattered small changes in a large file.

     ----iiggnnoorree--ffiillee--nnaammee--ccaassee
             Ignore the case of file names.

     ----nnoo--iiggnnoorree--ffiillee--nnaammee--ccaassee
             Do not ignore file name case.

     ----ssttrriipp--ttrraaiilliinngg--ccrr
             Skip identical lines.

     ----ttaabbssiizzee _N_U_M
             Change the size of tabs (default is 8.)

EENNVVIIRROONNMMEENNTT
     EDITOR, VISUAL
             Specifies an editor to use with the --oo option.  If both EDITOR
             and VISUAL are set, VISUAL takes precedence.  If neither EDITOR
             nor VISUAL are set, the default is vi(1).

     TMPDIR  Specifies a directory for temporary files to be created.  The de‐
             fault is _/_t_m_p.

